window.FERMI = [
  { n:"They are here and keeping their distance", k:"The zoo hypothesis", star:true,
    t:`Proposed by MIT radio astronomer John Ball in 1973. Advanced civilisations know about us and have set Earth aside like a wildlife reserve, watching without open contact until we reach some threshold. It predicts exactly what UFO history looks like: glimpses, deniability and the occasional intervention.`,
    x:`It needs every civilisation and every faction within them to honour the rule for millions of years. One defector breaks it.` },
  { n:"Something stops almost everyone", k:"The Great Filter",
    t:`Robin Hanson's 1996 argument. Between dead matter and a star-faring society there is at least one step that is nearly impossible. If it lies behind us we are rare. If it lies ahead, most civilisations destroy themselves, and nuclear weapons arrived suspiciously fast.`,
    x:`It says nothing about where the filter is, which is the only thing we want to know.` },
  { n:"Complex life is a fluke", k:"Rare Earth",
    t:`Ward and Brownlee, 2000. Microbes may be everywhere, but animals needed a large moon, plate tectonics, a giant planet to sweep up comets, and billions of quiet years.`,
    x:`We keep finding that conditions once thought special are common.` },
  { n:"Everyone is hiding", k:"The dark forest",
    t:`Any civilisation that reveals itself risks being destroyed by a neighbour that cannot be sure of its intentions. So the wise stay silent, and the loud do not last.`,
    x:`Earth has been broadcasting its biosphere through its atmosphere for two billion years, and nothing has come to burn it.` },
  { n:"The gaps are too wide", k:"Distance and time",
    t:`Civilisations may be common across cosmic time and still never overlap. A society that lasts ten thousand years is a single frame in a film thirteen billion years long.`,
    x:`Self-replicating probes could cross the galaxy in a few million years and would outlive their makers.` },
  { n:"We have barely looked", k:"The search is small",
    t:`One study compared the volume SETI has searched to a hot tub of water against all the world's oceans. Finding no fish in it tells you little.`,
    x:`Fair, and it cuts both ways. It is not evidence that anyone is there.` },
  { n:"There is no paradox", k:"Dissolving the question",
    t:`In 2018 Sandberg, Drexler and Ord ran the Drake equation with honest uncertainty ranges and found a substantial chance that we are alone in the galaxy, simply because several terms could be tiny.`,
    x:`It depends on how wide you draw the ranges for steps nobody has measured.` },
  { n:"They went somewhere we do not look", k:"Transcendence",
    t:`Advanced minds may turn inward to dense computation, other dimensions or the far cold future, and lose interest in stars and radio.`,
    x:`It asks us to accept visitors with no detectable presence, which is hard to tell apart from no visitors.` }
];

