window.PHYSICS = [
  { n:"Warp metrics", t:`In 1994 Miguel Alcubierre showed that general relativity allows a bubble of space to move faster than light while its contents sit still. It needs negative energy in amounts nobody knows how to make. Later designs cut the requirement but not to zero.` },
  { n:"Engineering the vacuum", t:`Hal Puthoff, a laser physicist who ran the CIA's remote viewing research, argues that inertia and gravity come from the quantum vacuum and could be altered. It is published and it is a minority view with no experimental support.` },
  { n:"Electrogravitics", t:`In the 1920s Thomas Townsend Brown found that charged capacitors seemed to push toward one plate. Mainstream tests find ion wind that disappears in a vacuum. The idea survives in the claim that the work went classified in the 1950s, a thread Jesse Michels has pursued in detail.` },
  { n:"Element 115", t:`Lazar said a stable isotope powers the discs. Moscovium was first made in 2003. Its known isotopes last less than a second. Theory allows an "island of stability" for heavier isotopes nobody has produced.` },
  { n:"The Navy patents", t:`From 2016 Navy engineer Salvatore Pais patented an "inertial mass reduction" craft and a compact fusion reactor, with a Navy chief technology officer vouching to the patent office. Released records show the Navy spent three years testing the core effect and saw nothing.` },
  { n:"The metamaterial sample", t:`A layered magnesium and bismuth fragment, said to come from a crash, was claimed to act as a waveguide for lifting craft. Oak Ridge National Laboratory tested it for AARO in 2024 and found an Earth-made alloy with no such property.` }
];

